exit: 0
--- stdout
commandments repent — Auto-fix sins — run every Scribe: the maintenance rewriters (Spatie Data hints) and each Repentable detector's own fix, backend and frontend.

Usage:
  commandments repent [path]        apply every fix, re-scanning until it converges (the default —
                                    this WRITES)
  commandments repent [path] --dry-run[=FILE]
                                    preview a unified diff instead, to the screen or a file
  commandments repent --only=NAME   run ONE rewriter (a Scribe or a Repentable detector, lenient
                                    name match)

Options:
  --changes                      only files changed in the working tree (alias: --git)
  --branch[=BASE]                only files new/changed on this branch vs BASE (default: main)
  --branch=REPO:[BASE..]HEAD     only files HEAD changed since it left BASE (default: main) in the
                                 repository REPO, a folder under the path with its own .git
  --pr=REPO#N                    only the files pull request N changes in the repository REPO, asked
                                 of GitHub with gh
  --repent[=ID]                  only the files listed in a past run's checklist (bare: the latest)
  --dry-run[=FILE]               preview the rewrite as a unified diff instead of applying it
  --only=NAME                    run one rewriter only (alias: --sin=NAME)
  --ignore-package-requirements  keep package-gated scribes even if this project lacks the package

  Rewrites only within the source roots `judge` reads (the config's paths), so it never touches
  tests/ or anything judge would not flag. A broken or incorrect repent result is a BUG — report
  it with `commandments report`, referencing both the source and the broken output.

  A rewriter that BREAKS is dropped so every other fix still applies — one bad scribe used to take
  the whole command down. The run then names it and exits 3, because it did not auto-fix everything
  it advertises.

